|~ `::regex` and what is declared under it: its constructor and its
   refusal, and `toRegex`, which answers the pattern itself. The words
   that read text by a pattern live under `::string`.
   The decisions it exists for: [D246]. ~|

:regex
  |~~ A pattern of text, `#"\d+ms"`, which the words of a string read text
      by: whether a match stands, `"took 12ms" | contains #"\d+ms"`, the
      first match and its groups, `match`, the groups it names, `capture`,
      the text of every match, `scan`, the text put in the place of every
      match, `replace`, and the pieces between the matches, `split`.

      What stands between the quotes reaches the engine as it is written:
      `\d` is a digit and `\.` a dot, with no backslash doubled, and `\"`
      is a quote that does not end the literal. The flags follow the
      closing quote, each once, `i` to ignore case, `m` for `^` and `$` at
      every line and `s` for a dot that matches a line break,
      `#"^error"im`. A pattern reads text by code points, as Unicode
      names characters, so `#"\p{L}+"` is a run of letters of any script.
      The dialect is the one of JavaScript in its edition of 2018, which
      every host reads alike: a group of flags, `(?i:…)`, and a group name
      written twice, which only later engines read, do not parse, nor does
      a pattern the engine cannot read, and the refusal says why, naming
      the repair of a habit of another dialect, flags written inside or a
      backslash before a character that needs none, `\-`, `\:`.

      A pattern is no string: `split "."` cuts a text at its dots, and
      `split #"[.,;]"` at any of three marks. Text becomes a pattern by
      `toRegex`, its flags the part of the call, `"error" | toRegex "i"`,
      and a pattern prints as its literal, which `print` writes. JSON
      writes that literal as a string, which `parse | apply it` reads back,
      and patterns rank after strings, by their text and then their flags.
      ::snippet~(::string | manifest)
      ::link~(::string/match | doc) ::link~(::string/capture | doc)
      ::link~(::string | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps |~~ A pattern, read as itself. ~~|
    | throws :PayloadNotRegexError
        |~~ `::regex` reads its payload as the pattern it is; text becomes
            one by `toRegex`, `"\\d+" | toRegex`.
        ~~|
    | proves :isAPattern
        |~~ `#"\d+"` is a pattern. ~~|
        ~(#"\d+" | type | eq ::regex)
    | proves :readsAsWritten
        |~~ A backslash reaches the engine as written, and `\"` writes a
            quote.
        ~~|
        ~(["12" "a.b" "say \"hi\""] | map ~(contains #"\d|\.|\"hi\"") | eq [true true true])
    | proves :takesItsFlagsAfterItsQuote
        |~~ The flags follow the closing quote and change how the engine
            reads.
        ~~|
        ~(["ERROR here" "a\nerror"] | get [~(get /0 | contains #"error"i) ~(get /1 | contains #"^error"m)] | eq [true true])
    | proves :readsLettersOfAnyScript
        |~~ A pattern reads text by code points, a class of Unicode among
            it.
        ~~|
        ~("данные 42" | match #"\p{L}+" | eq ["данные"])
    | proves :holdsOneDialect
        |~~ A group of flags and a group name written twice do not parse,
            on every host.
        ~~|
        ~(["#\"(?i:a)b\"" "#\"(?<y>a)|(?<y>b)\""] | map ~(parse !| type) | eq [::quote/ParseError ::quote/ParseError])
    | proves :namesTheRepairOfAHabit
        |~~ The refusal of a needless backslash names where a backslash
            stands.
        ~~|
        ~("#\"a\\:b\"" | parse !| get /message | contains "a backslash stands before")
    | proves :readsBackFromJson
        |~~ JSON writes a pattern as its literal, which `parse | apply it`
            reads back.
        ~~|
        ~(#"\d+"i | :p it | json | parseJson | parse | apply it | eq p)
    | proves :printsAsItsLiteral
        |~~ A pattern prints as its literal and reads back as itself. ~~|
        ~(#"\"(\d+)\""i | :p it | print | parse | apply it | eq p)
    | proves :isNoString
        |~~ A pattern is no string, so a text cuts at a dot and a pattern
            at any mark.
        ~~|
        ~("a.b,c" | get [~(split ".") ~(split #"[.,]")] | eq [["a" "b,c"] ["a" "b" "c"]])
    | proves :ranksAfterStrings
        |~~ Patterns rank after strings and before keywords, by their
            text.
        ~~|
        ~([:k #"b" "z" #"a"] | sort | eq ["z" #"a" #"b" :k])
    | proves :PayloadNotRegexError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string payload is refused; `toRegex` reads text as a
            pattern.
        ~~|
        ~(::regex"a" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::regex/PayloadNotRegexError ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:regex/toRegex
  |~~ Answers the pattern itself, so `map ~toRegex` turns patterns and
      the strings that hold their sources into patterns.
      ::link~(::string/toRegex | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The pattern itself. ~~| ::regex
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A pattern answers itself, so patterns and their sources turn
            into patterns.
        ~~|
        ~([#"\d" "\\d"] | map ~toRegex | eq [#"\d" #"\d"])
    | impl ::builtin{}

